WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.600
- This video is brought
to you by Storyblocks.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.730
- Hey, YouTube-iverse,

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:05.980
Neil deGrasse Tyson here.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.870
StarTalk up next,

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:11.468
Cosmic Queries: Planets and Stuff.

00:00:11.468 --> 00:00:14.051
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.755 --> 00:00:17.540
This is StarTalk

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:18.821
and I'm your host, Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:00:18.821 --> 00:00:20.780
your personal astrophysicist.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.600
And today is Cosmic
Queries: Planet Edition.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.830
But we don't just stick to planets.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.055
This one is Planets and Stuff.

00:00:29.055 --> 00:00:31.200
(laughs)

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.700
And today I've got Paul Mecurio with me.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.730
Paul.
- How are ya?

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:35.720
Good to see you.
- [Neil] Welcome back!

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.553
- Thank you.

00:00:36.553 --> 00:00:38.192
- [Neil] You're damn near a regular.

00:00:38.192 --> 00:00:39.897
- Please, that's all I want to do.

00:00:39.897 --> 00:00:41.400
- Now I didn't call you irregular.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.233
A regular,

00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:43.066
did I pronounce that?

00:00:43.066 --> 00:00:44.120
Did you hear?

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.530
- Actually I kind of am a little bit

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.040
but we can get into that later.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.150
I have a couple of questions
about how I can fix that

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:49.983
but we'll deal with that later.

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:51.160
- [Neil] Fix the irregularities?

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:51.993
- Exactly.

00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:52.826
- [Neil] Great to have you on this.

00:00:52.826 --> 00:00:54.470
- Thank you, so glad to be back.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.330
- And our normal format is
that we solicit inquiries

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.530
from our fan base on different platforms,

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.820
and the questions come in,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.160
I haven't seen them yet.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.950
This is not Stump Neil Day,

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:05.430
but if I don't know the answer

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:06.901
I'll say, "Next one please."

00:01:06.901 --> 00:01:07.910
(laughter)

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.900
- I've never seen you
have to say, "Next one".

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.550
- No, usually I'll have
something to say about it,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.110
even if I don't fully know the answer.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:13.960
- Right, exactly.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.960
- Just to tell people, did we
first meet at Stephen Colbert?

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.200
- We first met at the
Stephen Colbert Show--

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.220
- [Neil] 'Cause you're the warm-up guy.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.590
- Yeah, I warm-up on that show,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.427
I was a writer on the Daily Show.

00:01:23.427 --> 00:01:25.710
- Oh that goes even further back, yeah.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.797
- And then the Colbert Report

00:01:26.797 --> 00:01:29.260
and I just came to say
I was a big fan once,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:30.093
you were very nice,

00:01:30.093 --> 00:01:31.500
and then I came back the next time.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.081
I asked for a loan, you said no.

00:01:33.081 --> 00:01:36.873
Mister Big Shot doesn't
have any extra money, okay.

00:01:38.511 --> 00:01:40.960
And then you were nice
enough to come on my podcast

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.250
which has still gotten,
like, one of the top listens.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.083
It's--
- Really? Excellent.

00:01:45.083 --> 00:01:46.230
- Yeah.
- Excellent.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.096
- Dark matter and all this other stuff.

00:01:48.096 --> 00:01:49.869
- [Neil] Mm-hmm.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:51.570
- And then you asked me for money for that

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:52.896
and I'm like, that's inappropriate.

00:01:52.896 --> 00:01:54.562
(laughs) I don't have
any money, you have all-

00:01:54.562 --> 00:01:55.568
No, so yeah, so we go back.

00:01:55.568 --> 00:01:56.840
- [Neil] Yeah, yeah so that's great.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.673
It's great to have ya.

00:01:57.673 --> 00:02:00.960
And we are reporting now from YouTube,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.750
New York City in the Google building.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:03.583
- Yeah this is such a cool space.

00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:05.290
- At the YouTube production space.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:06.262
- Yeah.

00:02:06.262 --> 00:02:07.410
And so, they're kind enough to...

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.080
So I miss all my books behind me.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.180
Maybe I can have like a plastic version

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.400
and we'll just slide it in.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.707
- You could have them
Photoshopped in later.

00:02:14.707 --> 00:02:16.770
And it's right above the Chelsea Market.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.450
I got like a ham sandwich in my pocket.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:19.450
- Oh, yeah, you can't walk through there

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.410
without buying food.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.243
- I know, exactly.

00:02:21.243 --> 00:02:23.260
- I don't know why any skinny
people exist in this building.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.093
That's not possible.

00:02:24.093 --> 00:02:25.810
- There's actually a Weight
Watcher's office here,

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:26.658
that's pretty weird.

00:02:26.658 --> 00:02:28.573
(laughs) But yeah, it's great to be here,

00:02:28.573 --> 00:02:30.533
and it's a really cool space.
I didn't know this was here.

00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:31.560
- [Neil] Yeah, yeah.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.226
- And I always say, I'm
going to say it again,

00:02:33.226 --> 00:02:34.230
if I had you as a science teacher,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.380
I'd be doing science today.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.213
- Really, seriously?

00:02:36.213 --> 00:02:37.046
- I say that,

00:02:37.046 --> 00:02:37.911
I don't, I don't--

00:02:37.911 --> 00:02:38.830
right Carol?

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:39.961
Yeah that's my wife.

00:02:39.961 --> 00:02:43.550
Because you make it accessible,

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.756
your personality

00:02:45.756 --> 00:02:48.280
it comes, it brims, you're brimming with--

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.113
you love it.

00:02:49.113 --> 00:02:50.750
- You know what Carl Sagan said?

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.601
He said when you're in love,
you have to tell the world.

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:54.092
- Right.

00:02:54.092 --> 00:02:55.970
I had an 8th grade math teacher,

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.560
this big hulking guy,

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.576
he smoked in the class,

00:02:59.576 --> 00:03:01.430
and we made a battery.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.830
Like that was the big thing.

00:03:03.717 --> 00:03:05.330
"Now you put the thing over there..."

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:06.163
He's like this,

00:03:06.163 --> 00:03:07.860
it's like he was one step away

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.236
from going to off-track betting.

00:03:09.236 --> 00:03:12.383
And I just remembered
being intimidated by him,

00:03:12.383 --> 00:03:15.720
and I think people are very
intuitive from a young age

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.500
and can sense if somebody
cares or doesn't care.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.333
- [Neil] Oh, yeah.

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:20.230
- And he did not.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:21.312
That's why I think you're so great at it.

00:03:21.312 --> 00:03:22.145
- Okay well thank you.

00:03:22.145 --> 00:03:22.978
And just--

00:03:22.978 --> 00:03:23.811
- Look at me.

00:03:23.811 --> 00:03:24.644
I love you, man.

00:03:24.644 --> 00:03:25.477
- I love you man.

00:03:25.477 --> 00:03:26.387
It's not your fault.

00:03:26.387 --> 00:03:27.924
It's not your fault.

00:03:27.924 --> 00:03:28.825
(laughs)

00:03:28.825 --> 00:03:30.408
- It's me, it's me.

00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:34.365
- Just a little bit back at you,

00:03:34.365 --> 00:03:37.129
I deeply respect your profession.

00:03:37.129 --> 00:03:37.962
- Ahh.

00:03:37.962 --> 00:03:41.180
- The people who take
information in the world,

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.013
reshape it,

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:44.660
hand it back to you in
a fresh perspective,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.259
and make you laugh at it.

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.662
These are very special mirrors

00:03:48.662 --> 00:03:52.970
and I don't know how bearable
society would be without them.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:55.027
Without the comedian who
occasionally shows up and says,

00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:57.777
"Do you realize this is
what you've been saying?"

00:03:57.777 --> 00:03:59.730
"Oh my gosh, oh wow!"

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:00.563
- Right.

00:04:00.563 --> 00:04:03.390
- So this is why comedy is a
fundamental part of what we do

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:04.260
on StarTalk.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.132
- Well thank you,

00:04:05.132 --> 00:04:06.850
when I started on The Daily Show,

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.280
we were just this little show,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.340
and we were just doing jokes

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.173
and didn't really say,

00:04:11.173 --> 00:04:12.760
"Okay we're going to have
an impact on the world."

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:13.593
Or whatever, right?

00:04:13.593 --> 00:04:16.100
And then things started to happen

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.410
and we were always factually
based in our jokes.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.400
- [Neil] In the comedic-- yeah sure, sure.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.070
- And so you have to start with a premise.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:23.720
So we were delivering the news

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.990
with a non-biased point of view

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:26.823
and just saying,

00:04:26.823 --> 00:04:29.010
"Does this seem a little strange to you?"

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:29.940
So over time,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.077
people would come up to us and go,

00:04:32.077 --> 00:04:34.260
"I only get my news from The Daily Show."

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:35.093
Well first of all,

00:04:35.093 --> 00:04:35.926
we're a bunch of idiots,

00:04:35.926 --> 00:04:37.147
I don't think you should do that.

00:04:37.147 --> 00:04:37.980
Read some papers.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.510
- So you say, "What the
hell's wrong with you?"

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:40.343
- Exactly!

00:04:40.343 --> 00:04:41.176
(laughs)

00:04:41.176 --> 00:04:42.009
Read a couple of newspapers.

00:04:42.009 --> 00:04:43.700
It did end up becoming that,

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:44.533
and then you know,

00:04:44.533 --> 00:04:46.123
from that The Colbert Report,

00:04:46.123 --> 00:04:46.956
The Late Show,

00:04:46.956 --> 00:04:48.590
The Jon Oliver Show,

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:49.603
Sam Bee Show.

00:04:49.603 --> 00:04:50.670
- Yeah the whole universe.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.477
- Yes is all sort of
carrying that mantle of

00:04:54.477 --> 00:04:57.930
"Let's call people to the carpet,"

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.537
and so yeah, thanks.

00:04:59.537 --> 00:05:02.690
It was a nice by-product
of trying to have some fun.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.760
You get in so much trouble, too,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.397
because we would go too far with jokes,

00:05:06.397 --> 00:05:07.880
and the president of the
network would yell at us.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.760
But anyway, we'll talk
about that at another time.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.800
- So what do you got for me?
You got planet questions?

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:12.633
- I got some planet questions.

00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:14.787
I've got some very good
ones, some interesting ones.

00:05:14.787 --> 00:05:16.860
Questions and answers I want to hear.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.890
So let's start with
Patreon, his name is Ryan--

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.200
- Oh, yes I keep forgetting we do this

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.033
- He says--

00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:22.866
- We're kissing up to the Patreon members.

00:05:22.866 --> 00:05:24.080
- Yes exactly.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.541
- Ryan you are so handsome.

00:05:25.541 --> 00:05:27.260
(laughs)

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.560
That shirt you're wearing
looks fantastic on you.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.107
Write a check.

00:05:32.107 --> 00:05:34.880
"Is it true that if one
had an ocean large enough,

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.970
Saturn would float?"

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.823
- So this is often said,

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:42.870
I've said it,

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:44.810
colleagues of mine have said it.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.220
Let me restate that in a more
scientifically-precise way.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:50.053
- Okay.

00:05:50.053 --> 00:05:50.886
- Alright.

00:05:50.886 --> 00:05:53.220
Because technically that can't happen.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.510
You can't get a bathtub--

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.534
- It would be one hell of a bathtub.

00:05:56.534 --> 00:05:57.367
- It would,

00:05:57.367 --> 00:05:58.980
but I'll tell you why that
couldn't happen in a minute.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.210
So the scientifically
precise way to communicate

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:03.840
that same information

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.350
is that the average density of Saturn

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.053
is less than the density of water.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.063
And anything whose density is
less than water will float.

00:06:15.012 --> 00:06:15.845
- [Paul] Okay.

00:06:15.845 --> 00:06:16.678
- So Saturn--

00:06:16.678 --> 00:06:18.430
so the technical way to say that is,

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.120
if you scoop out a piece of Saturn,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.060
it will float on a puddle of water,

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:24.550
or in an ocean, it doesn't matter.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:25.846
So that's the way to say it.

00:06:25.846 --> 00:06:26.679
- Okay.

00:06:26.679 --> 00:06:27.957
- It's more fun to say,

00:06:27.957 --> 00:06:31.100
"Get an ocean or bathtub big
enough, Saturn will float".

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.416
And I've said that publicly,

00:06:34.416 --> 00:06:37.230
lamenting that when I was
a kid I knew this was true

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.063
about Saturn.

00:06:38.063 --> 00:06:38.896
- [Paul] Hmm.

00:06:38.896 --> 00:06:41.430
- And all I ever had in the
bathtub was a rubber ducky,

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.907
and I really wanted a rubber Saturn.

00:06:42.907 --> 00:06:45.300
(laughs)

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:46.690
So later in my life,

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:47.900
we come back to my office,

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.490
someone mailed me a rubber Saturn.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:50.323
- Is that right?

00:06:50.323 --> 00:06:51.763
- Yes, so now I have a rubber Saturn.

00:06:51.763 --> 00:06:52.596
- That's great.

00:06:52.596 --> 00:06:53.740
- And I can't-- I want
to leave it in my office,

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:55.490
I don't take it home to my bathtub.

00:06:57.257 --> 00:06:59.547
- "Honey, what's a rock
doing in the bathtub?"

00:06:59.547 --> 00:07:00.560
"It's a long story."

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:01.410
(laughs)

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.690
- So I've got a rubber Saturn.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:04.720
So the problem is,

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.860
if you had a lake bigger
than Saturn to put Saturn in,

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.190
the whole lake would collapse into Saturn.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.040
- Wouldn't the water just
spill over onto the shore,

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.800
when you displace the water?

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:16.633
- No, no, no.

00:07:16.633 --> 00:07:19.530
You're thinking that they exist
independently of each other.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.819
Saturn will pull the bathtub into it,

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:25.900
and it'll become a part of Saturn.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:27.896
- Oh, it's got the suction power.

00:07:27.896 --> 00:07:29.280
- Unless-

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.626
Well, yeah, it's called gravity.

00:07:31.626 --> 00:07:34.103
(laughs)
- Okay you didn't need to--

00:07:34.103 --> 00:07:34.936
- It's a little joke.

00:07:34.936 --> 00:07:35.769
- That's a little condescending,

00:07:35.769 --> 00:07:36.772
you didn't need to say it like that.

00:07:36.772 --> 00:07:37.605
- It's an old joke,

00:07:39.447 --> 00:07:41.914
"There's no such thing
as gravity, Earth sucks."

00:07:41.914 --> 00:07:42.747
- Nice.

00:07:42.747 --> 00:07:43.580
- That's the old joke.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:46.305
- That's funny.

00:07:46.305 --> 00:07:48.513
(laughs)

00:07:48.513 --> 00:07:49.518
- So what was the-

00:07:49.518 --> 00:07:50.782
where was I?

00:07:50.782 --> 00:07:53.069
So the problem is,

00:07:53.069 --> 00:07:56.470
you can't have a body of
water bigger than Saturn

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.303
that's flat,

00:07:57.303 --> 00:07:58.723
that you're going to put Saturn in.

00:07:58.723 --> 00:08:00.200
Because the body of water
would become a sphere,

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.033
and they'd collapse,

00:08:01.033 --> 00:08:02.780
they'd fall into each other.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.320
They'd each have mutual gravity,

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:05.630
they'll attract each other.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.470
It's not a do-able experiment,

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.200
but it does get the point across

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:10.870
that Saturn is lighter than water.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.470
- Plus Saturn would need a life jacket

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:13.360
in case something happened.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:14.520
- No, that's what the ring is for.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:15.380
- Oh!
- Ahh!

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.880
Gotcha on that one!

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:20.200
That's just a little life preserver.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.639
- I'm getting out of comedy now.

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:23.700
(laughs)

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:24.650
That's great.

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:25.750
- So it is in fact true,

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:27.710
it is the only planet
for which that is true.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.897
Every other planet would sink.

00:08:29.897 --> 00:08:31.610
- And it would absorb all of the water.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.830
- And by the way Jupiter is very gaseous,

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.710
and its density is close to that of water,

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.120
but just a little bit above it.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.563
- Now how do we know this about Saturn?

00:08:41.975 --> 00:08:42.808
- That's great.

00:08:42.808 --> 00:08:43.770
Do we go there?

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:45.436
No. So you know how big it is.

00:08:45.436 --> 00:08:46.269
So what is density?

00:08:46.269 --> 00:08:50.490
It's how much mass you have
in the volume that you occupy.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:53.590
So it's literally mass divided by volume.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:56.670
Grams per cubic centimeter,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.200
pounds per cubic inch.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.100
That's density, those
are the units of density.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.970
So we know the size of Saturn,

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:05.610
and we know the mass of Saturn.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.820
We can measure it from its
gravity and other things.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:09.430
You divide those two numbers,

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.330
you get a number that's less than one.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:13.133
- And that means...

00:09:13.133 --> 00:09:13.976
- In the right units.

00:09:13.976 --> 00:09:17.101
The density of water is one.

00:09:17.101 --> 00:09:17.934
- [Paul] Oh!

00:09:17.934 --> 00:09:18.840
- Yeah, by definition, basically.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:19.673
- [Paul] Ahh, okay.

00:09:19.673 --> 00:09:20.506
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:20.506 --> 00:09:22.020
- So we know.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:23.300
- Yeah, it'll float.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:24.133
- Alright.

00:09:24.133 --> 00:09:27.400
- And, um, here's something
that I figured out about myself

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:28.670
just a couple weeks ago,

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:30.370
I was swimming a couple weeks ago,

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:32.590
and I was floating more easily

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:34.273
than ever I have in my life.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:36.570
- Did you-- you had a lot of hair?

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:37.542
You needed a hair cut?

00:09:37.542 --> 00:09:38.375
- No.

00:09:38.375 --> 00:09:40.377
(laughs) No, I'm just a
little chubbier than I was

00:09:40.377 --> 00:09:42.020
when I was younger.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:43.910
Because fat floats and muscle sinks,

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:44.743
that's the thing.

00:09:44.743 --> 00:09:45.920
It's about the density.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.790
How much mass are you
packing into a volume?

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:48.670
- So why do you think--

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:51.580
- You could say, "How
much does a whale weigh?"

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.519
A whale weighs nothing,

00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:58.519
it's neutrally buoyant
swimming in the water.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.260
You have to ask, "How
much mass does it have?"

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:06.690
It's why the biggest
animals that ever existed,

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:08.003
exist in the water.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:10.900
They don't have to hold
up their own weight

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:14.630
against the gravity on Earth's surface.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:18.120
The whale is the biggest
creature that ever existed

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:19.530
in the history of life on Earth.

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:21.000
- And yet it can float.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.000
- Because its density is
about the same as water.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:26.690
If you are less than water,

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.480
it would have to use
energy to stay under water,

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.250
because the water would
want to make it float.

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:32.830
- Which happens to human beings.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:34.501
- Well if you're chubbier,

00:10:34.501 --> 00:10:36.443
your body will float.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:39.370
Will float very well.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.130
And you'll take energy to go down below.

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:44.460
If you are more dense than water,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:45.550
you will sink to the bottom,

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:47.750
it'll take energy to stay afloat.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:49.860
If you're about the same density as water,

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.036
you can move through water with no-

00:10:52.036 --> 00:10:54.630
like the ease of a trapeze,

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.848
or however that line goes in the song.

00:10:56.848 --> 00:11:00.380
So following up on that,

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:01.667
here's something interesting, ready?

00:11:01.667 --> 00:11:02.500
- Mmmhmm.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.000
- You know who the smartest
person in the world was?

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:05.833
You're raising your hand?

00:11:05.833 --> 00:11:06.734
No.

00:11:06.734 --> 00:11:07.650
(laughs)

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:09.460
One of the most clever people in the world

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:13.580
was the first person to figure
out you can build a ship

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:16.820
and everything you make the ship out of

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:19.790
is denser than water,

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:20.683
and it can float.

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:26.410
You can build a ship out
of steel and have it float.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:27.630
- That does blow me away when I see it,

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:28.911
when I see a cruise ship--

00:11:28.911 --> 00:11:31.573
- It changed warfare.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.270
- And was it a specific
scientific experiment--

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:36.589
- Can you pause on that sentence, please?

00:11:36.589 --> 00:11:39.610
It changed warfare.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:43.020
You don't have to build
your boat out of wood.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:43.853
- Right.

00:11:43.853 --> 00:11:46.693
- Which is susceptible to cannonball fire.

00:11:46.693 --> 00:11:48.923
You can build it out of steel.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:52.230
How do you get it to float?

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:56.540
Because the part of the
boat that sits below water

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.193
is mostly air.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:02.930
The hull of the ship sits below water.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:06.620
So what matters is not that
steel is heavier than water

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:07.560
and air is lighter than water,

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.920
its what is the average density

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:13.840
of that which you have
plunked in the water?

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.990
It's the total volume
divided by the total mass.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:18.210
It has less mass than you think,

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:19.663
because most of it is air.

00:12:20.518 --> 00:12:21.770
An aircraft carrier,

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:24.320
most of its volume is air.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:25.690
- So it's able to float,

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:28.232
because of the relation
of density to the water.

00:12:28.232 --> 00:12:29.800
- Correct.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:32.220
Anything that's floating
is less dense than water,

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:33.085
on average.

00:12:33.085 --> 00:12:34.120
Period.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.340
- And do you think that
that was by mistake?

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:37.700
A byproduct of something else,

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:39.830
that someone figured out
that steel can float?

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:41.620
- No, they were making canoes out of wood,

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:42.453
because wood floats,

00:12:42.453 --> 00:12:44.116
and why would you make
it out of anything else?

00:12:44.116 --> 00:12:44.949
- Right.

00:12:44.949 --> 00:12:45.782
But then the steel-

00:12:45.782 --> 00:12:46.615
the next step was steel.

00:12:46.615 --> 00:12:47.448
- In order for it to float,

00:12:47.448 --> 00:12:50.120
it has to have a significant
amount of volume under water.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.293
So that the air can represent this.

00:12:53.142 --> 00:12:56.797
- So the larger the vessel,
the deeper the hull,

00:12:56.797 --> 00:12:57.630
for the most part.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:58.463
- Generally.

00:12:58.463 --> 00:12:59.840
It's not going to just be skimming,

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.470
it's going to sink in,

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.813
it's going to respond to the
fact that there is steel there,

00:13:04.813 --> 00:13:08.380
come down to the level that's just right

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:09.299
for what the weight is.

00:13:09.299 --> 00:13:11.410
If you look at container ships,

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:13.347
they have these lines painted
on the side of the hull,

00:13:13.347 --> 00:13:16.370
depending on how much
gross weight it's carrying.

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:17.870
The more weight it's carrying,

00:13:18.807 --> 00:13:21.810
the heavier the thing is,

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:23.220
the total density goes up,

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:25.400
and it's going to sink a
little lower into the water.

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:26.329
- Got it.

00:13:26.329 --> 00:13:29.246
They know when they've
reached their max capacity.

00:13:29.246 --> 00:13:31.817
- Yeah, so, ice is denser than cork.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:37.000
- That's counter-intuitive in
looking at ice versus cork.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.210
- Well cork is from a tree,
so you know it'll float,

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:42.060
but it's less dense because ice-

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.070
evidence of that is

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:45.930
the cork will float almost
entirely above the water.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.400
There's hardly anything sunk below it,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:49.350
whereas ice,

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:50.920
90 percent of it is below the water.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:53.560
- Oh, which is why you have icebergs.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:55.320
- That's why you have the iceberg problem.

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:56.560
Yeah.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.530
And James Cameron in
Titanic, he did it right.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:00.363
Okay?

00:14:00.363 --> 00:14:01.196
- [Paul] Did he?

00:14:01.196 --> 00:14:02.029
- Basically.

00:14:02.029 --> 00:14:02.862
When they hit the iceberg,

00:14:02.862 --> 00:14:04.283
it's this little thing in the water.

00:14:04.283 --> 00:14:05.940
It's a little thing.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:07.139
- [Paul] Sticking out of the surface.

00:14:07.139 --> 00:14:07.972
- Just a little thing sticking-

00:14:07.972 --> 00:14:09.880
90 percent of it is underwater!

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.340
That's what cut up the side of the hull.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:13.173
Oh my gosh.

00:14:13.173 --> 00:14:15.713
Whereas in the first Titanic movie,

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:20.300
the black-and-white, 1940s, whatever,

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:22.466
there's this huge iceberg out there.

00:14:22.466 --> 00:14:24.368
"Oh my gosh!"

00:14:24.368 --> 00:14:25.885
(laughs)

00:14:25.885 --> 00:14:27.513
- Then it has a hand that
reaches out and cuts the hull.

00:14:27.513 --> 00:14:28.856
- [Neil] It cuts it!

00:14:28.856 --> 00:14:30.522
(laughs)
- [Paul] It's got a knife.

00:14:30.522 --> 00:14:31.355
- It's in a knife fight.

00:14:31.355 --> 00:14:33.409
- It's like West Side Story in the ocean.

00:14:33.409 --> 00:14:35.274
♪ Once a Jet, always a Jet ♪

00:14:35.274 --> 00:14:36.107
(laughs)

00:14:36.107 --> 00:14:40.189
(sings musical notes in unison)

00:14:40.189 --> 00:14:41.570
- So if you do the physics right,

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:42.810
you don't need a big iceberg,

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.070
and in fact this has contributed
to one of several reasons

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:48.110
why it was so hard to notice.

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:49.360
It was actually small.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:50.620
- Dark, it was foggy.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:52.040
- Dark, there wasn't any moon.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.310
It wasn't foggy, but it was-

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:56.074
- Right, someone was watching
YouTube on their phone.

00:14:56.074 --> 00:14:57.270
(laughs)

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:58.380
- We're going to move
on, that's fantastic--

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.512
- Oh my favorite anachronistic quote?

00:15:00.512 --> 00:15:01.345
- [Paul] Hmm?

00:15:01.345 --> 00:15:03.405
- Is a quote from Abraham Lincoln,

00:15:03.405 --> 00:15:04.238
where he said,

00:15:04.238 --> 00:15:06.537
"Never trust anything
you see on the internet."

00:15:06.537 --> 00:15:08.620
(laughs)

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:11.898
It's a meme, you've gotta find it.

00:15:11.898 --> 00:15:12.997
- That's hilarious.

00:15:12.997 --> 00:15:15.268
- It's so beautiful,
you've just gotta love it.

00:15:15.268 --> 00:15:18.802
- And coming from him, he's
got a lot of credibility.

00:15:18.802 --> 00:15:19.750
- Yeah he does.

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:21.416
(laughs)
- [Paul] I'll give it to you-

00:15:21.416 --> 00:15:22.627
- That's a man that knew what
the hell he was talking about.

00:15:22.627 --> 00:15:23.790
- The stovepipe hat,

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:25.553
ehh, questionable choice there.

00:15:26.608 --> 00:15:27.974
That and a buckle on a hat.

00:15:27.974 --> 00:15:29.050
- [Neil] Yeah?

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.000
- What do pilgrims need
a buckle on a hat for?

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:32.110
- He wasn't a pilgrim.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:33.440
Excuse me, this was 200 years later.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:34.582
Would you get your time periods straight.

00:15:34.582 --> 00:15:35.415
- [Paul] I understand that--

00:15:35.415 --> 00:15:36.530
- It was a top-hat without a buckle.

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:38.620
- This is why my brain goes this way.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.620
Answer that question, smarty pants!

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:41.970
Why do you need a buckle on a hat?

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:43.050
Is somebody letting their head out?

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:45.879
- Well no, you can squeeze it
down, in case the wind blows.

00:15:45.879 --> 00:15:46.712
- Ahh.

00:15:46.712 --> 00:15:47.704
But the wind

00:15:47.704 --> 00:15:48.537
and the density

00:15:48.537 --> 00:15:49.370
and the volume

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:51.036
and the whale...
- Uh oh I taught you too much!

00:15:51.036 --> 00:15:52.350
(laughs)

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:53.183
- You've created a-

00:15:53.183 --> 00:15:55.550
The student has become the master.

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:57.410
Alright, you're going to eat
that cookie later I gave you,

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:58.243
right?

00:15:58.243 --> 00:16:00.996
- I got a quick one, since
we're talking about hat brims.

00:16:00.996 --> 00:16:01.829
- [Paul] Okay.

00:16:01.829 --> 00:16:04.330
- Um do you know the size of
your own hat that you wear?

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:06.487
- I have a pea head. I have
a pea-brained pea head.

00:16:06.487 --> 00:16:08.420
I'm like 7 1/2, small.

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:09.392
I have a small head.

00:16:09.392 --> 00:16:12.300
- 7 1/2 would not be a small
hat, that's a large hat.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:13.714
- Don't argue with me about my hat size.

00:16:13.714 --> 00:16:15.486
- So you've got a fat head if
that's your actual hat size.

00:16:15.486 --> 00:16:16.319
- No, no--

00:16:16.319 --> 00:16:17.430
- No, no no--

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:19.096
- 6 and then- it's
probably 6 1/2, it's small.

00:16:19.096 --> 00:16:20.160
- 6 1/2 it could be, so here's--

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:21.903
- Your head.
- I have a fat head.

00:16:21.903 --> 00:16:22.800
- Yeah.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:24.170
It's a building is really what it is.

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:25.560
But anyway go ahead.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:27.530
- So I don't know if you knew this,

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:30.703
but that hat size has
mathematical significance.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:32.693
Okay?

00:16:32.693 --> 00:16:34.780
- Please tell me it's not in
relation to my you-know-what?

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:35.980
- No, it's not.

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:37.620
Not that I know that you know what I know

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:39.469
with what you said you know what.

00:16:39.469 --> 00:16:40.302
(laughs)

00:16:40.302 --> 00:16:41.276
So here's what you do.

00:16:41.276 --> 00:16:42.109
You take a tape measure

00:16:42.109 --> 00:16:44.650
and measure the
circumference of your head.

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:46.290
Write down that number.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.369
Divide it by pi,

00:16:48.369 --> 00:16:50.530
that's your hat size.

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:51.363
- Oh!

00:16:51.363 --> 00:16:52.196
- Yeah.

00:16:52.196 --> 00:16:53.540
I just thought it was that!

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:54.532
- No, no, no.

00:16:54.532 --> 00:16:56.480
- Oh you've gotta divide by pi.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:58.742
- If the circumference of
your head is 6 1/2 inches,

00:16:58.742 --> 00:17:00.380
you're some other species.

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:01.700
(laughs)

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:03.361
You're a Rhesus monkey, I don't know!

00:17:03.361 --> 00:17:05.170
(laughs)

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:06.470
- Well my wife would agree with you

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:08.050
about some other species.

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:09.390
You don't want to see
me with my clothes off,

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:10.460
it's not pretty.

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:14.110
Um okay, so I just learned another thing,

00:17:14.110 --> 00:17:15.540
because I just thought my head size

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:16.443
was literally like you just measure--

00:17:16.443 --> 00:17:17.880
- No, no, no, no, no.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:20.070
You must have never really put
those two numbers together.

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:21.500
So I was in a hat shop,

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.480
because I always wanted to own a top hat

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:24.930
that you could pop into--

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:25.763
- [Paul] Oh yeah!

00:17:25.763 --> 00:17:26.596
- I always wanted one,

00:17:26.596 --> 00:17:27.429
and they make hats,

00:17:27.429 --> 00:17:29.750
so they measured my fat
head, and they wrote it down.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:31.030
They said, "You need a size eight,"

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:31.863
or something.

00:17:31.863 --> 00:17:32.754
So I said,

00:17:32.754 --> 00:17:33.587
"Fine, do you know where
you get this number?"

00:17:33.587 --> 00:17:34.420
He said he didn't know,

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:35.253
and I told him.

00:17:35.253 --> 00:17:36.086
He said, "Really? Really?"

00:17:36.086 --> 00:17:36.919
And so we did it,

00:17:36.919 --> 00:17:39.170
and I'm glad he was skeptical

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:40.350
so then you can do the experiment.

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:41.343
We did it on his head,

00:17:41.343 --> 00:17:42.482
and his assistant's head,

00:17:42.482 --> 00:17:43.940
it all came out!

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:45.262
It was all perfect.

00:17:45.262 --> 00:17:46.990
He wanted me to write it out,

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:48.930
so I wrote it out and I signed it,

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:50.432
and it's on the wall of his hat shop.

00:17:50.432 --> 00:17:51.265
- That's fantastic!

00:17:51.265 --> 00:17:52.931
- Yes, yes. With the
pi and the arithmetic--

00:17:52.931 --> 00:17:54.587
- Can you imagine him going home,

00:17:54.587 --> 00:17:55.767
"Honey how was work?"

00:17:55.767 --> 00:17:58.760
"Well this dude came in, and
we did math problems all day.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:02.196
But I sold a $400 hat, so
I guess it was a good day!"

00:18:02.196 --> 00:18:04.666
That's so awesome, you signed
it and it's on his wall.

00:18:04.666 --> 00:18:05.499
- Signed it,

00:18:05.499 --> 00:18:06.332
it's on his wall.

00:18:06.332 --> 00:18:07.165
It's on the wall.

00:18:07.165 --> 00:18:07.998
- That's better than like a diner

00:18:07.998 --> 00:18:09.926
that gets you to sign a
picture of yourself up there--

00:18:09.926 --> 00:18:11.170
- Why would you want a picture of me?

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:12.925
No you want to put
something that can advance

00:18:12.925 --> 00:18:15.271
human understanding of the universe.

00:18:15.271 --> 00:18:17.001
- That came from your
giant, ghoulish head.

00:18:17.001 --> 00:18:19.820
(laughs)

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:21.710
- Oh now ghoulish is
part of the adjective.

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:24.220
- This is my last time
doing the show by the way.

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.777
- Okay we ran out of time
to do a second question

00:18:26.777 --> 00:18:27.660
for this segment.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.326
