00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:50.010
So you can't have the same name--

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:50.843
- [Neil] Still?

00:19:50.843 --> 00:19:52.490
- So I dropped the first R in my name.

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:53.323
- [Neil] So Mecurio.

00:19:53.323 --> 00:19:55.094
- And people still
think I'm him sometimes.

00:19:55.094 --> 00:19:58.700
He's hosting a reality
beer show in Australia.

00:19:58.700 --> 00:19:59.533
- [Neil] That's a thing, okay.

00:19:59.533 --> 00:20:02.480
- And so, yeah, he makes like
craft beer, and so every--.

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:03.313
- Craft or crap?

00:20:03.313 --> 00:20:04.205
(laughs)

00:20:04.205 --> 00:20:06.450
Missed the last syllable.
- No, there's an F in there.

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:07.283
Craft.

00:20:07.283 --> 00:20:08.280
- Craft.

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.530
- And I get, every two weeks or so,

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:12.100
I get beer recipes from people

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:13.712
thinking I'm him.

00:20:13.712 --> 00:20:15.340
I'm like okay that's not me-

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:17.050
so anyway, @paulmecurio is my thing.

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:18.160
- Alright, go for it.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:20.293
- Robert Weaver, also Patreon.

00:20:21.187 --> 00:20:24.370
"Thanks very much for continuing
to educate and inspire us.

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:27.340
What news in science do you
find the most interesting

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:28.490
or important?

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:31.040
Is it the private space race?"

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.321
- No, I know too much about
the private space race

00:20:33.321 --> 00:20:36.570
for that to be uniquely interesting to me.

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:39.410
Things that interest me are
things I know little about.

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:40.330
And so-

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:42.160
that I want to know more about.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:45.770
I love watching the
progress of neuroscience.

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:47.050
I think the brain,

00:20:47.050 --> 00:20:49.000
our inner universe,

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:52.210
in its attempt to contemplate
the outer universe,

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:54.840
presents one of the greatest
intellectual challenges

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:56.390
of our modern times.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:59.518
- And what advancements
are we making right now

00:20:59.518 --> 00:21:02.330
that either surprised you or--

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:04.370
- My view of the field,

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:07.780
which is a very unpopular
view among psychologists,

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:10.650
is that the day will come when-

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:12.070
I'm hypothesizing this-

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:13.006
that the day will come

00:21:13.006 --> 00:21:17.030
when we understand the
neurosynaptic phenomena in the brain

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:18.343
with such precision,

00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:21.500
that no one will ever have
to lay down on a couch

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:24.610
and spend an hour with a psychologist.

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:26.547
- Because I do that.

00:21:26.547 --> 00:21:30.030
And I, seriously, there's a lot
of stuff I gotta figure out,

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:30.863
work out.

00:21:30.863 --> 00:21:31.776
- Right, and some of it-

00:21:31.776 --> 00:21:33.970
maybe there's-

00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:36.388
- Let me tell you about my mother, so-

00:21:36.388 --> 00:21:37.430
I'm joking.

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:39.410
- So there may be things

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:40.890
where people just need someone to talk to,

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:41.730
that's one thing.

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:43.140
But if you have some trauma,

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:46.130
or some issue that you can't shake,

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:50.833
or some addiction you can't overcome,

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:53.590
I'm imagining there's a day we'll find

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:55.570
the exact spot of the brain
that's responsible for it.

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:59.848
That prevents you from pushing
that next glass of Hi-ball

00:21:59.848 --> 00:22:01.100
away from you.

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:03.710
- Or to go into depression,
or whatever it might be.

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:04.543
- Oh, depression!

00:22:04.543 --> 00:22:05.562
Especially depression,

00:22:05.562 --> 00:22:10.350
or anything else that
lands you in one of these--

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:11.640
- [Paul] Funky states or whatever.

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:13.120
- One of these hospitals,

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:14.010
in these mental hospitals.

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:16.523
So you say, "That's the
spot that's misfiring."

00:22:16.523 --> 00:22:17.950
- So what are we doing to the spot?

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:19.290
Are we going going in and operating,

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:20.790
are we taking a medication--

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:21.770
- At this point, who knows?

00:22:21.770 --> 00:22:24.270
Is it too much firing or
is it not firing enough?

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:26.736
There has to be another path into it

00:22:26.736 --> 00:22:28.890
that connects to this other brain center

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:30.010
that helps modulate it.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:30.843
I don't know.

00:22:30.843 --> 00:22:32.730
I'm imagining

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:34.400
that a future will arrive

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:39.110
where we have that much
knowledge and control

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:40.370
of what's going on in the brain,

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:42.210
so that you can just nip and tuck

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:43.780
and fix all of these problems.

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:46.180
- Why wouldn't psychologists
welcome this theory?

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:48.460
- Because they'd all go
out of business overnight.

00:22:48.460 --> 00:22:50.240
Because you don't go into
anybody's office for an hour

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:52.280
and talk to them. No
excuse me, not for an hour.

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:55.340
For a whole suite of sessions
that could go on for years.

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:57.113
That gets resolved in an afternoon.

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:01.140
- I think my psychologist
is making up a lot of stuff.

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:02.023
I think I'm fine.

00:23:02.023 --> 00:23:03.280
(laughs)

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:04.113
I really do.

00:23:05.950 --> 00:23:06.990
- It's just a whole community.

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:10.100
There's counselors, and
psychologists, psychiatrists,

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:11.100
this sort of thing.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:12.810
We're kind of almost there.

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:16.390
There's a lot of depression
that is not cured but addressed

00:23:16.390 --> 00:23:18.150
by medication.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.610
We know what chemicals will
leave you in a depressed state,

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:21.700
which ones don't.

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:22.533
And why.

00:23:22.533 --> 00:23:24.130
Is it the production of those chemicals?

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:27.360
Well lets go in, adjust your genome,

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:31.290
or you neurosynaptic drivers,

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.660
in such a way that you're
producing your own chemicals

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:34.900
so you don't need the pill.

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:37.250
- Right, because the problem
with some of these pills,

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:39.190
is they're just sort of
Whack-a-Mole, you fix one problem,

00:23:39.190 --> 00:23:41.486
and then there's sort
of byproduct symptoms

00:23:41.486 --> 00:23:42.450
of problems--

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:44.490
- Correct, because the pill gets ingested

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:46.120
and it goes into your whole system,

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:49.260
whereas if you surgically go
into one part of the brain--

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:50.093
so now,

00:23:50.093 --> 00:23:51.910
one of the challenges to that future

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:55.027
is the brain is highly
interconnected to itself.

00:23:55.027 --> 00:23:55.860
- [Paul] Okay.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:24:00.580
- So an ability expressed
in one part of the brain,

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:03.070
some of that is also in
other parts of the brain,

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:05.388
which is how you can compensate for-

00:24:05.388 --> 00:24:06.660
Some brain injuries--

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:07.727
- It's not compartmentalized.

00:24:07.727 --> 00:24:10.360
- Not as compartmentalized
as we'd want it to be

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:11.853
for it to be a nice clean thing.

00:24:11.853 --> 00:24:13.074
- [Paul] Right, right, right, right.

00:24:13.074 --> 00:24:13.920
- Right, right.

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:14.850
And while we're on the subject,

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:17.140
did you see the movie...

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:20.448
What's the one with Scarlet
Johansson, where she-

00:24:20.448 --> 00:24:22.340
where her brain power's increased-

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:23.173
- Yeah I can't remember the name.

00:24:23.173 --> 00:24:24.550
- [Neil] Is it Lucy?

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:26.140
- I think it's Lucy, yeah.

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:27.080
- Lucy, yeah.

00:24:27.080 --> 00:24:29.860
So the entire premise of that film

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:32.790
is that she takes some drug,

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:33.840
turns out by accident,

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:37.200
but it boosts her brain power.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:38.800
Not to something she didn't,

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:39.633
or any of us,

00:24:39.633 --> 00:24:40.890
don't already have.

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:45.070
It just enabled her to
access the "90% of the brain

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:46.200
that you're not using",

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:47.033
okay?

00:24:47.033 --> 00:24:48.550
Where did this whole premise come from?

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:50.230
And by the way there are
whole scenes in this film

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:52.680
of rooms full of psychologists,

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:54.662
studying her as a phenomenon.

00:24:54.662 --> 00:24:55.890
Okay.

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:58.900
It comes from some guy a
hundred years ago or so,

00:24:58.900 --> 00:24:59.733
I forgot his name,

00:24:59.733 --> 00:25:00.566
forgive me,

00:25:00.566 --> 00:25:03.930
who through brain injuries
and other aspects of-

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:05.770
cause you can't just go
in and start poking around

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:06.886
in someone's brain--

00:25:06.886 --> 00:25:07.719
- You can't?

00:25:07.719 --> 00:25:08.552
(laughs)

00:25:08.552 --> 00:25:09.817
- You sound like you have been.

00:25:09.817 --> 00:25:11.350
"I've got to stop that.

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:12.487
Cross that one off my list."

00:25:12.487 --> 00:25:13.691
- I've got a guy in my basement.

00:25:13.691 --> 00:25:14.524
- You've got a guy.

00:25:14.524 --> 00:25:17.120
- I've got to probably
let him go now, alright.

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:19.840
- So what he said was,

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:20.720
what he published was,

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:22.820
the brain is so complex,

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:27.560
we know only what ten
percent of it is used for.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:28.393
- But how is he--

00:25:28.393 --> 00:25:29.370
- Excuse me!

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:30.803
That became-

00:25:32.337 --> 00:25:34.037
"We only use ten percent of our brain".

00:25:34.037 --> 00:25:35.430
- But how do we know he's right?

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:36.300
It's a theory.

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:37.153
- No, no.

00:25:37.153 --> 00:25:39.900
You're in the wrong room right now.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:40.760
- Alright, I'll leave then.

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:42.364
- No, what he said--

00:25:42.364 --> 00:25:44.067
- No I'm done, I'm leaving.

00:25:44.067 --> 00:25:44.980
- "I can't work like this."

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:47.170
- There's a band playing downstairs.

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:48.831
- There is!

00:25:48.831 --> 00:25:50.031
The YouTube music space.

00:25:51.010 --> 00:25:54.117
It doesn't matter whether
or not he's accurate,

00:25:54.117 --> 00:25:55.740
and he probably was accurate.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:58.410
He said, "Well there's
the language center here,

00:25:58.410 --> 00:25:59.243
and there's another-"

00:25:59.243 --> 00:26:00.219
All he said was,

00:26:00.219 --> 00:26:01.800
"The brain is so complex,

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:06.800
we only know what ten
percent of it is used for."

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:08.640
That's all he said.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:10.650
A completely innocent comment.

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:13.112
We have much more to
learn about the brain.

00:26:13.112 --> 00:26:18.112
It got re-quoted as, "We only
use ten percent of our brain."

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:21.250
- Oh, so that's how I know it.

00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:22.083
- This is my point.

00:26:22.083 --> 00:26:24.520
You weren't paying attention to what I'm-

00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:25.820
Okay--

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:26.653
- Sorry teacher.

00:26:26.653 --> 00:26:29.880
- If you think we use only
ten percent of your brain,

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:34.193
this is the mantra for
teachers with students.

00:26:35.210 --> 00:26:39.030
It's very hard thing to correct,

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:43.170
when saying it incorrectly
is what you want to hear.

00:26:43.170 --> 00:26:44.760
- Why do you think we want to hear that?

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:47.120
- Because we want to believe
that we all have potential

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:48.576
we can grow into.

00:26:48.576 --> 00:26:50.085
- Oh.

00:26:50.085 --> 00:26:52.077
- Okay, here's the underachieving student.

00:26:52.077 --> 00:26:53.770
"You're just using ten percent.

00:26:53.770 --> 00:26:56.400
Keep working on using more of your brain,

00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:58.124
you have vastly more potential."

00:26:58.124 --> 00:26:58.957
- [Paul] Right.

00:26:58.957 --> 00:27:02.230
- We're into potentiality,
especially in America.

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:04.580
- Okay now I'm going to teach
you how to tie your shoe again

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:06.376
and you're six and seven years old,

00:27:06.376 --> 00:27:08.140
you can do more.

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:10.827
- So that then became the
premise of this entire movie.

00:27:10.827 --> 00:27:12.477
"She's now using 40
percent of her brain..."

00:27:12.477 --> 00:27:13.900
"She's now using 60 percent percent..."

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:17.900
And at 80 percent she's,
like, flying through webpages

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:20.750
learning whole dictionaries
in a matter of minutes,

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:23.360
and she's even changing her
hair color with her brain,

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:24.680
and starts doing-

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:25.513
telekinetically.

00:27:25.513 --> 00:27:26.346
Which is weird, like,

00:27:26.346 --> 00:27:28.926
why should a smarter person
be able to move things

00:27:28.926 --> 00:27:31.010
