00:31:21.486 --> 00:31:24.170
- "Why doesn't the matter
in Saturn's rings accumulate

00:31:24.170 --> 00:31:28.247
like the matter in a protoplanetary disk?"

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:30.033
I know the answer to this.

00:31:30.033 --> 00:31:31.820
Because it gets cleaned once a week

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:33.650
by a guy with a ShopVac.

00:31:33.650 --> 00:31:34.483
Anyway, go ahead.

00:31:34.483 --> 00:31:35.364
- No, that's not it.

00:31:35.364 --> 00:31:38.120
So Saturn's rings are very thin,

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:39.600
and they're small particles,

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:41.050
and they're very thin.

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:43.490
And the matter does collect.

00:31:43.490 --> 00:31:47.120
It collects into certain zones,
clearing out other zones.

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:47.953
So in fact,

00:31:47.953 --> 00:31:50.860
Saturn's rings have gaps in them.

00:31:50.860 --> 00:31:52.350
- Which you kind of see, right?

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:55.640
- You can see with a
medium-power backyard telescope.

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:58.150
In fact it's not just simply a gap,

00:31:58.150 --> 00:32:01.370
you can see the ball of
Saturn through that gap

00:32:01.370 --> 00:32:02.510
when it's at the right angle.

00:32:02.510 --> 00:32:03.850
So you know there are no particles there,

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:05.150
or at least very few.

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:07.040
So they collect, not in a sphere,

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:08.607
but they collect orbitally.

00:32:08.607 --> 00:32:12.723
Because they have stable
orbits around Saturn,

00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:15.940
and there's not much
mass there to begin with,

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:17.880
they're not gonna overcome this orbit

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:21.245
and then coalesce into
one moon, for example.

00:32:21.245 --> 00:32:24.530
When our moon was created, we had a ring.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:27.030
That whole ring collapsed into a moon,

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:30.220
because of huge chunks of
material that were there.

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:32.100
Whose gravity wins?

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:33.570
It's winner takes all.

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:37.160
If you have slightly more
gravity than this other object,

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:39.650
you will attract slightly
more material than this will.

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:41.090
- And those larger objects,

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:42.920
in what was the ring around the moon,

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:44.437
came from just sort of meteors--

00:32:44.437 --> 00:32:45.870
- Earth had a collision.

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:48.501
We were side-swiped by another planet.

00:32:48.501 --> 00:32:49.334
- Right.

00:32:49.334 --> 00:32:53.420
- And our Earth's crust got
spewed into orbit around us,

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:54.253
into a ring.

00:32:54.253 --> 00:32:56.750
And that ring then
coalesced into the moon.

00:32:56.750 --> 00:32:59.230
And so there you have it.

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:02.010
So one of the most depressing
things that I learned,

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:02.843
because I'm not an Orbital Dynamicist,

00:33:02.843 --> 00:33:05.890
