00:36:56.700 --> 00:36:57.533
they were not-

00:36:57.533 --> 00:36:59.532
It's not the Bolshoi.
- Once you got it, you got it.

00:36:59.532 --> 00:37:01.600
(laughs)

00:37:01.600 --> 00:37:03.565
- But that was a chapter
of my life, long gone.

00:37:03.565 --> 00:37:04.490
- Okay.

00:37:04.490 --> 00:37:06.277
- I'm not thinking of my
present and future and saying,

00:37:06.277 --> 00:37:08.076
"Gee, I should dance again."

00:37:08.076 --> 00:37:11.160
These are not my thoughts.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:13.377
When I was dancing, I
was not writing books.

00:37:13.377 --> 00:37:16.251
No one was publishing my essays, okay?

00:37:16.251 --> 00:37:18.859
(laughs)

00:37:18.859 --> 00:37:22.510
So, Paul, take kindly
the counsel of the years.

00:37:22.510 --> 00:37:25.228
Gracefully surrendering
the things of youth.

00:37:25.228 --> 00:37:27.210
(laughs)

00:37:27.210 --> 00:37:28.259
- I think you should dance.

00:37:28.259 --> 00:37:30.180
(laughs)

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:31.307
- After all that I just told you,

00:37:31.307 --> 00:37:32.261
that's the best you could--

00:37:32.261 --> 00:37:34.311
- Just listen to what I tell you, please.

00:37:35.521 --> 00:37:39.810
So you have a, possibly a failed star.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:41.840
- Oh sorry, that's what
we were talking about.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:42.673
Thank you.

00:37:42.673 --> 00:37:43.506
- Yeah.

00:37:43.506 --> 00:37:45.923
- So Jupiter, had it been-

00:37:45.923 --> 00:37:49.010
and, you know, I forgot
the latest numbers on this-

00:37:49.010 --> 00:37:54.010
had it been maybe five times more massive,

00:37:54.210 --> 00:37:56.470
somewhere around between five and ten,

00:37:56.470 --> 00:37:57.730
it would've been a star.

00:37:57.730 --> 00:37:59.960
We would've had a double-star system.

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:02.300
- Why does that size
suddenly make it a star?

00:38:02.300 --> 00:38:04.450
- Because the bigger you are,

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:07.360
the higher the pressure is in your core.

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:09.296
Because there's more mass,

00:38:09.296 --> 00:38:11.360
there's more weight,

00:38:11.360 --> 00:38:12.773
there's more pressure.

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:16.050
When you have pressure in the core,

00:38:16.050 --> 00:38:18.090
the temperature rises.

00:38:18.090 --> 00:38:20.550
As the temperature rises
there's a threshold

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:25.000
where you start fusing nuclei

00:38:26.110 --> 00:38:27.513
in the presence of heat.

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:32.480
Thermo-nuclear fusion.

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:34.370
That's where you get that word from.

00:38:34.370 --> 00:38:37.520
The sun is undergoing
thermonuclear fusion in its core.

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:39.720
The high pressures, high temperatures,

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:41.780
are squeezing together hydrogen atoms,

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:44.570
and out the other side comes helium atoms,

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:47.570
and the helium atom has less
mass than the hydrogen atom.

00:38:47.570 --> 00:38:48.823
Where did the mass go?

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:51.630
E=mc2, it became energy.

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:54.350
And that energy is sustaining the star.

00:38:54.350 --> 00:38:56.501
Jupiter didn't have enough.

00:38:56.501 --> 00:38:58.440
It didn't hit that threshold.

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:00.650
- And forgive me if this
sounds like a silly question,

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:01.975
and I'm being serious.

00:39:01.975 --> 00:39:04.790
If Jupiter's 90 percent hydrogen--

00:39:04.790 --> 00:39:06.280
- [Neil] Yes.

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:07.890
- Is it just this ball of gas?

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:10.330
Could I fly through it, or would I hit?

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:11.910
- Yeah, so there's no surface.

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:13.397
Right, so you fly straight into it

00:39:13.397 --> 00:39:15.220
and you just keep plunging down,

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:16.240
but you would start feeling

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:18.270
higher and higher gas pressure above you,

00:39:18.270 --> 00:39:20.650
and eventually you would get squashed.

00:39:20.650 --> 00:39:21.483
Or vaporized,

00:39:21.483 --> 00:39:23.130
depending on whether you hit a hot zone

00:39:23.130 --> 00:39:25.340
sooner than a high-pressure zone.

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:28.840
So it depends on how
resistant to pressure you are

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:30.160
that could get you to deeper-

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:31.610
if you have a vessel for example-

00:39:31.610 --> 00:39:34.799
that could get you to deeper
places where it's even hotter.

00:39:34.799 --> 00:39:36.706
So Jupiter did get hot at it's core,

00:39:36.706 --> 00:39:37.913
oh don't get-

00:39:37.913 --> 00:39:41.320
let's not be unclear about this-

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:42.560
it did get hot.

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:45.150
It just didn't get hot
enough to hit that threshold

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:46.700
of thermonuclear fusion.

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:48.330
It would've been fun
to have two suns though

00:39:48.330 --> 00:39:49.900
in our solar system.

00:39:49.900 --> 00:39:51.910
- What would that have been like for us?

00:39:51.910 --> 00:39:52.791
- [Neil] Bad.

00:39:52.791 --> 00:39:53.950
(laughs)

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:54.970
- A lot of sunscreen.

00:39:54.970 --> 00:39:57.440
- As you go around the suns,

00:39:57.440 --> 00:39:59.460
you're closer to one at one point,

00:39:59.460 --> 00:40:00.680
and not so close to the other.

00:40:00.680 --> 00:40:04.440
That can wreak havoc on the
stability of your climate.

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:07.940
Also it wreaks havoc on the
stability of your orbit.

00:40:07.940 --> 00:40:10.490
Where is your orbital allegiance today

00:40:10.490 --> 00:40:11.940
relative to yesterday?

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:13.846
Never good for orbits.

00:40:13.846 --> 00:40:15.410
Your orbits go unstable,

00:40:15.410 --> 00:40:18.320
and you fall into one of the
two stars or you get flung out.

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:19.380
- I say that all the time,

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:20.741
it's never good for two orbits,

00:40:20.741 --> 00:40:22.917
never good for orbits.

00:40:22.917 --> 00:40:23.810
(laughs)

00:40:23.810 --> 00:40:24.664
- Next question.

00:40:24.664 --> 00:40:25.863
- Next question, okay.

00:40:25.863 --> 00:40:30.863
This is Ulysses Lindblad, Twitter.

00:40:30.957 --> 00:40:32.560
"Do you get high and low tide

00:40:32.560 --> 00:40:35.210
on the northern and southern poles?"

00:40:35.210 --> 00:40:38.330
- Ooh very good question.

00:40:38.330 --> 00:40:40.030
You know I never thought about that.

00:40:40.030 --> 00:40:41.830
So let me think about it
now for the first time.

00:40:41.830 --> 00:40:42.853
And I'm thinking no.

00:40:43.810 --> 00:40:44.643
- [Paul] Why?

00:40:44.643 --> 00:40:45.870
- Because the bulge-

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:46.910
Well, excuse me,

00:40:46.910 --> 00:40:49.420
you wouldn't, definitely wouldn't,

00:40:49.420 --> 00:40:53.190
in the first day of spring
and first day of fall.

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:54.560
Because on those two days,

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:58.833
the poles are sideways to Earth.

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:04.930
And the tidal bulge is
a bulge of the ocean,

00:41:05.310 --> 00:41:07.280
of the water mass of the Earth,

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:08.130
and the tidal bulge

00:41:08.130 --> 00:41:12.750
it bulges both towards the
moon and away from the moon.

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:14.925
So it bulges in both directions.

00:41:14.925 --> 00:41:15.870
- Moving this way.

00:41:15.870 --> 00:41:16.830
- This way, correct.

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:19.770
That's why there are two
high tides in a day, right?

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:21.690
Do you know, did you ever--

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:23.110
- Oh yeah I know,

00:41:23.110 --> 00:41:23.943
we go boating.

00:41:23.943 --> 00:41:24.776
- [Neil] You go boating.

00:41:24.776 --> 00:41:25.609
There are two high tides in a day.

00:41:25.609 --> 00:41:26.442
- Why, why- I didn't even know that.

00:41:26.442 --> 00:41:27.460
- Because it has two bulges.

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:29.530
One- so here's the moon off to the side,

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:30.900
there's a bulge that's
closest to the moon,

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:32.580
and a bulge that's opposite the moon.

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:33.413
So you say, well,

00:41:33.413 --> 00:41:34.480
if the moon is tugging on it,

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:36.150
how could it bulge the other way?

00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:37.490
Here's what's happening,

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:40.970
the moon is tugging on the
water and on the Earth.

00:41:40.970 --> 00:41:42.010
So it's tugging-

00:41:42.010 --> 00:41:44.110
the closest water gets pulled the most.

00:41:44.110 --> 00:41:45.173
- Makes low tide.

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:47.670
- Only on the sides of this,

00:41:47.670 --> 00:41:51.300
because the water got sucked
out of the intermediate places

00:41:51.300 --> 00:41:52.133
on Earth.

00:41:52.133 --> 00:41:54.510
The point that points towards the moon,

00:41:54.510 --> 00:41:55.343
that's closest,

00:41:55.343 --> 00:41:57.240
it feels the moon's gravity the greatest.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:42:00.060
Then it's Earth that
feels it next-greatest.

00:42:00.060 --> 00:42:01.360
Then there's the side of the water,

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:02.800
on the other side of the Earth,

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:04.590
that feels it the least.

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:08.453
So there's an elongation
of the entire system

00:42:08.453 --> 00:42:10.738
in the direction of the moon.

00:42:10.738 --> 00:42:11.571
- And that's called--

00:42:11.571 --> 00:42:13.930
- These are called tidal
forces, tidal stretching.

00:42:13.930 --> 00:42:14.784
Earth has tidal-

00:42:14.784 --> 00:42:16.630
goes through tides.

00:42:16.630 --> 00:42:17.930
They're not as high as the water,

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:18.763
but Earth,

00:42:18.763 --> 00:42:20.040
the solid mass of Earth,

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:22.030
actually stretches in this way.

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:23.730
As Earth turns once a day,

00:42:23.730 --> 00:42:25.850
it goes through two high tides.

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:28.190
Now these bulges come out

00:42:28.190 --> 00:42:30.670
from one side of the Earth and the other.

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:31.530
Okay.

00:42:31.530 --> 00:42:35.850
So the size of your tides are
affected by several things.

00:42:35.850 --> 00:42:38.820
But also, your latitude, yes.

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:39.653
On Earth.

00:42:39.653 --> 00:42:43.840
I'm expecting that the
equator will have higher range

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:45.360
between high and low tides

00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:46.880
than other latitudes would.

00:42:46.880 --> 00:42:49.120
But there are other
factors that affect it.

00:42:49.120 --> 00:42:50.810
You know the highest swinging tides

00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:52.610
is the Bay of Fundy.

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:53.443
- Why?

00:42:53.443 --> 00:42:54.500
- Bay of Fundy,

00:42:54.500 --> 00:42:56.510
because there's a long-

00:42:56.510 --> 00:42:57.490
is it a fjord?

00:42:57.490 --> 00:43:01.870
It's a very long track of land

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:05.053
that two sides of the
land that has a long-

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:07.249
I think it's a fjord.

00:43:07.249 --> 00:43:09.750
And what happens is,

00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:14.750
the tide comes in and the
slope is very shallow.

00:43:17.890 --> 00:43:20.040
So if the tide goes up a foot,

00:43:20.040 --> 00:43:21.857
it goes in a half a mile.

00:43:21.857 --> 00:43:22.850
- Oh!

00:43:22.850 --> 00:43:23.840
- It's really cool,

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:25.970
you can like run alongside the tide

00:43:25.970 --> 00:43:27.033
as the tide is coming in.

00:43:27.033 --> 00:43:27.983
- Because it's so shallow.

00:43:27.983 --> 00:43:28.900
- It's so shallow,

00:43:28.900 --> 00:43:30.600
and so the tide, when it goes up a foot,

00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:31.760
it's going to go up a foot.

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:33.020
But if to go up a foot,

00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:36.420
it makes you have to go down-shore a mile,

00:43:36.420 --> 00:43:39.620
that's where it's going to
be when it goes up a foot.

00:43:39.620 --> 00:43:41.310
So the movement of these tides

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:42.680
creates friction against-

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:44.640
between the waters and the Earth.

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:47.680
And the height of the tide
I think is not so important

00:43:47.680 --> 00:43:48.513
in that case,

00:43:48.513 --> 00:43:51.120
as much as how much the tide moves,

00:43:51.120 --> 00:43:54.267
how much friction Earth feels against it.

00:43:54.267 --> 00:43:56.880
You know, this friction
between the Earth and the tides

00:43:56.880 --> 00:43:58.703
is slowing down Earth's rotation.

00:43:59.610 --> 00:44:00.443
- Latitude.

00:44:00.443 --> 00:44:02.465
- We're in the Stuff
part of this broadcast.

00:44:02.465 --> 00:44:03.410
(laughs)

00:44:03.410 --> 00:44:05.640
- Why is it slowing down the rotation?

00:44:05.640 --> 00:44:06.530
- Because Earth-

00:44:06.530 --> 00:44:08.750
oh, I left something out.

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:11.160
This tidal bulge that I
said aligns with the moon?

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:11.993
It doesn't.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:15.330
- Don't lie to me, man. (laughs)

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:16.163
- Don't play with me.

00:44:16.163 --> 00:44:18.290
- I have to lie to begin the explanation.

00:44:18.290 --> 00:44:19.200
Otherwise there's too-

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:20.102
it's TMI.

00:44:20.102 --> 00:44:21.890
(laughs)

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:23.573
Do we understand what I just told you,

00:44:23.573 --> 00:44:26.180
that the moon stretches this whole system?

00:44:26.180 --> 00:44:27.413
- First of all, stop yelling at me.

00:44:27.413 --> 00:44:28.500
No, I'm just kidding.

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.600
Um yes, and can I just
say it quickly for my own-

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:33.910
So we're on the East
coast, the Atlantic Ocean.

00:44:33.910 --> 00:44:35.680
And we're in New York City.

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:37.460
So when this side of
the Earth that we're on

00:44:40.750 --> 00:44:42.778
is sort of aligned with the moon,

00:44:42.778 --> 00:44:44.440
that's when you're going to get low tide,

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:45.319
because it's pulling--

00:44:45.319 --> 00:44:46.440
- No if we're aligned with the moon,

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:48.050
it pulls it and we're
going to get high tides.

00:44:48.050 --> 00:44:49.650
- I'm sorry, it's pulling it, right.

00:44:49.650 --> 00:44:50.483
Okay I got it.

00:44:50.483 --> 00:44:51.530
- Now, you want to know
something interesting?

00:44:51.530 --> 00:44:52.847
When you're at the beach, you say,

00:44:52.847 --> 00:44:55.000
"Oh the tide's coming
in, tide's going out."

00:44:55.000 --> 00:44:56.900
That's not what's happening.

00:44:56.900 --> 00:44:59.990
The tide is just always there in space.

00:44:59.990 --> 00:45:03.630
You're on the solid Earth
and you're rotating through

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:05.086
the high and low tides.

00:45:05.086 --> 00:45:05.919
- Oh!

00:45:08.070 --> 00:45:10.850
So if I picked a spot in the ocean,

00:45:10.850 --> 00:45:13.220
the tide is always that level?

00:45:13.220 --> 00:45:14.420
- No, not a spot in the ocean.

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:17.319
You pick a spot in line
between the Earth and the moon,

00:45:17.319 --> 00:45:18.880
pick that spot,

00:45:18.880 --> 00:45:22.380
that will always be water
at a certain height.

00:45:22.380 --> 00:45:23.213
It'll just be there.

00:45:23.213 --> 00:45:25.250
And so, that whole system,

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:29.561
and Earth, the solid
Earth, is rotating in that.

00:45:29.561 --> 00:45:31.357
- And so when we're in it,
it feels like high tide,

00:45:31.357 --> 00:45:32.259
and when we're not--

00:45:32.259 --> 00:45:34.930
- When we're in it, we say
it's tide coming in and out,

00:45:34.930 --> 00:45:38.550
but it's Earth rotating
into a high-tide bulge

00:45:38.550 --> 00:45:40.276
and out of a high-tide bulge.

00:45:40.276 --> 00:45:41.460
- [Paul] Wow.

00:45:41.460 --> 00:45:42.720
- Yeah.

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:43.553
- Does the water get--

00:45:43.553 --> 00:45:44.386
- Pretty cool.

00:45:44.386 --> 00:45:45.219
- Does the water get displaced--

00:45:45.219 --> 00:45:46.052
- It's the illusion

00:45:46.052 --> 00:45:48.013
that something's coming towards
us and away from us. It is-

00:45:48.013 --> 00:45:48.887
you are rotating into it.

00:45:48.887 --> 00:45:49.900
And so now,

00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:51.210
I said it aligns with the moon?

00:45:51.210 --> 00:45:52.428
It doesn't.

00:45:52.428 --> 00:45:53.923
It's ahead of the moon.

00:45:55.100 --> 00:45:55.933
- And why is that?

00:45:55.933 --> 00:45:58.550
- Because the Earth is dragging the tide.

00:45:58.550 --> 00:46:00.440
This whole bulge wants
to align with the moon,

00:46:00.440 --> 00:46:01.640
but Earth is not letting it.

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:03.010
It's pulling it in front.

00:46:03.010 --> 00:46:06.610
So the tidal bulge is ahead of the moon.

00:46:06.610 --> 00:46:08.026
- It's gravitational pull right?

00:46:08.026 --> 00:46:08.859
- [Neil] Uh, it's--

00:46:08.859 --> 00:46:10.413
- See, I didn't use the word suck!

00:46:10.413 --> 00:46:11.710
(laughs)

00:46:11.710 --> 00:46:14.570
- It's the friction between
Earth and all of this water

00:46:14.570 --> 00:46:19.340
that is shoving the water
ahead of where the moon is.

00:46:19.340 --> 00:46:22.397
The moon is trying to
resist this by pulling-

00:46:22.397 --> 00:46:23.817
"Come back to me tides!"

00:46:23.817 --> 00:46:25.670
"No, I'm pulling it ahead of you."

00:46:25.670 --> 00:46:28.110
This battle between Earth and the moon

00:46:28.110 --> 00:46:29.823
is slowing down Earth's rotation.

00:46:31.330 --> 00:46:34.930
It is the source of the
occasional leap-second

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:37.240
that is thrown into the calendar.

00:46:37.240 --> 00:46:40.100
- Because it's slowed
down by this process.

00:46:40.100 --> 00:46:41.420
- Because the moon is making the tides,

00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:44.800
and the tides are resisting
what Earth is trying to do.

00:46:44.800 --> 00:46:47.890
- And what would Earth be
like if that did not happen?

00:46:47.890 --> 00:46:49.249
- [Neil] If we didn't have tides?

00:46:49.249 --> 00:46:50.082
- If we-

00:46:50.082 --> 00:46:52.020
if it didn't get slowed down

00:46:52.020 --> 00:46:53.973
by this friction that's happening?

00:46:55.362 --> 00:46:57.430
- Uh, if we didn't get slowed down,

00:46:57.430 --> 00:47:00.850
then the duration of
a day would be stable.

00:47:00.850 --> 00:47:01.683
But it's not.

00:47:01.683 --> 00:47:03.620
The length of the day is increasing.

00:47:03.620 --> 00:47:04.957
Very slowly, you're
not going to notice it,

00:47:04.957 --> 00:47:06.450
and you're not gonna care.

00:47:06.450 --> 00:47:07.550
But clocks care.

00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:09.150
GPS satellites care.

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:10.910
That matters.

00:47:10.910 --> 00:47:11.743
- A day isn't 24-

00:47:11.743 --> 00:47:13.395
Every day isn't 24 hours,
is what you're saying?

00:47:13.395 --> 00:47:14.630
- No, not anymore.

00:47:14.630 --> 00:47:16.550
It's slightly longer than 24 hours.

00:47:16.550 --> 00:47:19.300
And rather than increase
the length of a second,

00:47:19.300 --> 00:47:21.200
which would create it's own problems,

00:47:21.200 --> 00:47:22.033
we just say,

00:47:22.033 --> 00:47:24.820
"Wait 'til that increase
accumulates a second,

00:47:24.820 --> 00:47:26.430
then we just throw one in."

00:47:26.430 --> 00:47:27.263
- [Paul] That's the leap-second.

00:47:27.263 --> 00:47:30.570
- So on the leap-second years,

00:47:30.570 --> 00:47:34.563
the last minute of that
year has 61 seconds in it.

00:47:35.455 --> 00:47:38.300
Just to make up for things.

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:39.970
Because of the tides.

00:47:39.970 --> 00:47:42.220
Because we are dragging ourselves through

00:47:42.220 --> 00:47:44.025
high tides and low tides in space.

00:47:44.025 --> 00:47:46.480
- This explains why I'm always late.

00:47:46.480 --> 00:47:49.330
The tides, thank you. I
got to the bottom of it.

